atrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -(-1) & -2\\end{bmatrix},$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-2$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-1.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2+(-1)\\text{i}=-2-\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3+0\\text{i} \\\\ 5+(-2)\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2∓\\text{i},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5∓2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186362360 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362360 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362360 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362360 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186362360 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "36d2e97b-bfbd-5b5a-8af5-ecab59e35588" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362360 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 0\\end{bmatrix},$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0+(-2)\\text{i}=-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}4+1\\text{i} \\\\ -3+(-1)\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4+\\text{i} \\\\ -3-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =∓2\\text{i},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ -3∓\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186362361 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362361 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362361 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362361 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186362361 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33cf532b-66bb-5817-8de3-f7bd5092526d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362361 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186362369 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362369 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362369 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362369 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1+6\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1-5\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186362369 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "975ead56-2d77-565d-8ac3-aceac4612c80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362369 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=1+5\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1-6\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186362371 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362371 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362371 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362371 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186362371 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb6d22f5-37c8-5d1a-9e4f-59c3f6e96912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362371 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1+\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(2-\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186368509 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368509 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368509 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368509 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368509 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368509 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368509 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368509 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186368511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368509 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f9dc9bc4-ccb6-5db4-bf02-998de4352520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368510 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368510 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368510 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fa069ff5-4e98-53b3-b07f-bc15b801bdce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368511 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368511 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368511 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eb846609-83f1-56ec-9241-fb062e5a40d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368512 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417]